<?php declare(strict_types=1); ?><!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="description" content="Project Unveiled puts the identity of Jesus on trial by separating early evidence from later tradition."><title>Who Was Jesus? | Project Unveiled — Truth on Trial</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="/truth/truth-worthy.css"></head><body><header class="topbar"><div class="wrap nav"><a class="brand" href="/truth/">PROJECT <span>UNVEILED</span></a><nav class="navlinks"><a href="/truth/">Truth on Trial</a><a href="/truth/#ask">Ask a Question</a><a href="/book/research.html">Research Standards</a></nav></div></header><main><section class="hero"><div class="wrap"><div class="eyebrow">TRUTH ON TRIAL · FLAGSHIP CASE 000003 · POWERED BY TRUST-WORTHY</div><h1>WHO WAS JESUS — BEFORE RELIGION TOLD US WHO HE WAS?</h1><p>This trial asks a narrower, harder question than “What do Christians believe?” We separate claims that can be historically established from theological interpretation, later tradition, and conclusions that require faith.</p><div class="manifesto"><strong>Free Finding:</strong> The earliest surviving Christian evidence does not present Jesus merely as a later religious invention. It places him in first-century Judea, associates him with named contemporaries, preserves his execution under Roman authority, and shows an unusually early movement convinced that his death did not end the story. That establishes a historical core. It does not, by itself, prove every theological claim made about him. <strong>YOU BE THE JUDGE.</strong></div></div></section><section class="section"><div class="wrap grid"><article class="card"><div class="case-head"><div><div class="case-id">PU-TOT-000003</div><h2>The Claim on Trial</h2></div><div class="status">OPEN</div></div><p><strong>Claim:</strong> Jesus of Nazareth was a real first-century Jewish teacher whose life and execution generated the movement that became Christianity; the question of who he ultimately was requires evaluating layers of historical evidence and theological claims separately.</p><h2>What is strongly grounded historically</h2><ul class="rules"><li>Jesus belongs to the historical setting of first-century Roman Judea rather than appearing first in a much later period.</li><li>Paul's undisputed letters are early Christian documents and already refer to Jesus' crucifixion, followers and family connections.</li><li>The canonical Gospel traditions preserve overlapping claims about Jesus while also differing in wording, arrangement and emphasis.</li><li>Non-Christian ancient references provide limited external corroboration that Jesus/Christ and his followers were known in antiquity; these sources must be weighed individually rather than treated as independent proof of every Gospel claim.</li></ul><h2>What history alone cannot simply prove</h2><ul class="rules"><li>That a miracle occurred merely because an ancient source reports it.</li><li>That Jesus is divine merely because followers called him divine.</li><li>That resurrection belief was false merely because resurrection is extraordinary.</li><li>That later church doctrine is identical in every respect to Jesus' own vocabulary and emphasis.</li></ul><h2>The pressure point</h2><p>The strongest version of the investigation is not “Jesus existed, therefore Christianity is true,” nor “miracles are impossible, therefore Christianity is false.” Both shortcuts smuggle the conclusion into the premise. The real work is chronological: identify the earliest recoverable claims, determine how close each source is to the events, test dependence and contradiction, distinguish testimony from interpretation, and ask which explanation best accounts for the surviving evidence.</p><div class="bobinated"><div class="eyebrow">Bobinated Opinion — clearly labeled opinion</div><p>The evidence appears substantially stronger for a historical Jesus than for the claim that Jesus was invented wholesale generations later. The harder question is not whether somebody named Jesus stood behind the movement; it is whether the extraordinary conclusions his followers reached about him best explain what happened next. That question deserves investigation rather than either automatic belief or automatic dismissal.</p></div><div class="judge">YOU BE THE JUDGE.<small>PROJECT UNVEILED · POWERED BY TRUST-WORTHY</small></div></article><aside class="card"><div class="eyebrow">DEEP DIVE</div><h2>This is the free finding.</h2><p>The full investigation is designed to go further: source chronology, Pauline material, Gospel relationships, Josephus and Tacitus, resurrection claims and alternatives, Jesus' own reported sayings, development of Christology, contradictions, manuscript questions, strongest skeptical case, strongest Christian case, and exactly what evidence would change the finding.</p><div class="finding"><strong>Status:</strong> Full evidence dossier in development.<br><strong>Confidence in historical core:</strong> High.<br><strong>Confidence in supernatural conclusions:</strong> Not reducible to the same historical confidence scale without additional philosophical and theological premises.</div><p><a class="button" href="/truth/#ask">Challenge This Trial / Submit Evidence</a></p><p class="muted">Trust-Worthy does not ask you to accept a verdict because an institution, skeptic, preacher, fact-checker or AI said so. Inspect the evidence trail.</p></aside></div></section></main><footer class="footer"><div class="wrap"><strong>PROJECT UNVEILED</strong> · Truth on Trial · Powered by Trust-Worthy · <strong>YOU BE THE JUDGE.</strong></div></footer></body></html>